bability of loss but a larger loss. The reason prefers more right-skewed — i.e., more weight on unfavorable outcomes — so it would prefer A. But the definition says 'greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes', so A has more probability on unfavorable outcomes (0.2% vs 0.1%), so A is more right-skewed. Therefore, the decision reason can be applied: it compares the probability mass on negative outcomes, and prefers the one with more such mass. Thus, the reason is applicable to this decision problem.",
